%I A054540 #16 Nov 08 2026 20:53:28
%S A054540 1,2,3,5,7,12,19,31,34,53,118,171,289,323,441,612,730,1171,1783,2513,
%T A054540 4296,12276,16572,20868,25164,46032,48545,52841,73709,78005,151714,
%U A054540 229719,537443,714321,792326,944040,1022045,1251764,3755292,3985011
%N A054540 A list of equal temperaments (equal divisions of the octave) whose nearest scale steps are closer and closer approximations to the six simple ratios of musical harmony: 6/5, 5/4, 4/3, 3/2, 8/5 and 5/3.
%C A054540 The sequence was found by a computer search of all of the equal divisions of the octave from 1 to over 3985011. There seems to be a hidden aspect or mystery here: what is it about the more and more harmonious equal temperaments that causes them to express themselves collectively as a perfect, self-accumulating recurrent sequence?
%C A054540 From _Eliora Ben-Gurion_, Dec 15 2022: (Start)
%C A054540 The answer is because temperament mappings can be added. If harmonic correspondences are written in a bra, that is <N2 N3 N5], where Nx is the step corresponding to the x-th harmonic, then these types of one-row matrices can be added and the resulting temperament will represent them as well. In case of temperaments with high precision, this also leads to another high-precision temperament. Such a bra notation is referred to as "val" by the microtonal music community, and in simple words, vals can be added together to produce another val.
%C A054540 Example: a tuning with 118 equal steps to the octave has a second harmonic on the 118th step by definition, the third harmonic is approximated with 187 steps, and the fifth is with 274 steps, which leads to <118 187 274]. A 171 equal division system will have a corresponding bra <171 271 397]. When these two are added, we obtain <289 458 671], which is exactly how the 2nd, 3rd, and 5th harmonics are represented in 289 equal divisions of the octave. (End)
%C A054540 From _Hal M. Switkay_, Oct 25 2026: (Start)
%C A054540 The entry as originally written does not define a metric for evaluating the closeness of several approximations simultaneously. We endeavor to fill this gap.
%C A054540 Let a = log_2(4/3), b = log_2(5/4), c = log_2(6/5). These numbers represent the fraction of an octave occupied by the perfect fourth, the major third, and the minor third. We have a + b + c = 1. 1-a, 1-b, and 1-c correspond to the perfect fifth (3/2), the minor sixth (8/5), and the major sixth (5/3). These are the six intervals referenced in the title.
%C A054540 We seek simultaneous rational approximations of a, b, and c, all with the same denominator. Call these approximations p/s, q/s, and r/s. We hope for consistency, meaning that p + q + r = s. Rational approximations of a, b, and c as above lead to corresponding approximations for 1-a, 1-b, and 1-c, with the same denominator. It suffices to focus on a, b, and c.
%C A054540 Let s be a natural number. Let p(s) (respectively q(s), r(s)) be the result of rounding a*s (respectively b*s, c*s) to the closest integer. Compute the RMSE of the approximations.
%C A054540 RMSE(s) = sqrt(((a-p(s)/s)^2 + (b-q(s)/s)^2 + (c-r(s)/s)^2)/3)
%C A054540 Then the terms of this sequence are the values of s where RMSE(s) attains a new record minimum.
%C A054540 Notes: Consistency fails for the cases s = 1 and s = 2. Also, 53 is the last practically feasible equal division of the octave with the property that a(n+1) > 2*a(n). (End)
%H A054540 Tonalsoft - Encyclopedia of Microtonal Music Theory, <a href="http://tonalsoft.com/enc/v/val.aspx">Val - a linear functional on the vectors in the tonespace lattice</a>.
%H A054540 Xenharmonic Wiki, <a href="https://en.xen.wiki/w/Val">Val</a>
%F A054540 Stochastic recurrence rule - the next term equals the current term plus one or more previous terms: a(n+1) = a(n) + a(n-x) + ... + a(n-y) + ... + a(n-z), etc.
%e A054540 34 = 31 + the earlier term 3. Again, 118 = 53 + the earlier terms 34 and 31.
%Y A054540 Cf. A001149, A018065, A001856, A002858, A007335, A060525, A060526, A060527.
